#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199470 :tutorial/content "Let's now derive a general formula for the flux through an *upward-oriented* Cartesian surface.\n\nFor a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\cdot (\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nConsider a surface $S$ given by the equation $z=g(x,y).$ The canonical parameterization of the surface is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(x,y) = \\left\\langle x,\\: y,\\: g(x,y) \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nComputing the tangent vectors to the grid curves, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'} & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂g}{∂x}⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'} & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂g}{∂y}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & \\frac{∂g}{∂x} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix}=⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the $\\mathbf k$-component is positive, which indicates that our surface is upward-oriented.\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(x,y))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize as follows:\n\n- *For a surface $S$ given by $z = g(x,y)$ for $(x,y) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle P,\\: Q,\\: R \\right\\rangle$ over $S$ is given by*\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left( -P\\dfrac{\\partial g}{\\partial x} - Q\\dfrac{\\partial g}{\\partial y} + R \\right) \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe have analogous results for surfaces of the form $y = h(x,z)$ and $x=k(y,z){:}$\n\n- *For a surface $S$ given by $y = h(x,z)$ for $(x,z) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle P,\\: Q,\\: R \\right\\rangle$ over $S$ is given by*\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left( -P\\dfrac{\\partial h}{\\partial x} + Q - R\\dfrac{\\partial h}{\\partial z} \\right) \\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\n*where the unit normal to the surface is chosen such that the $\\mathbf j$-component is positive.*\n\n- *For a surface $S$ given $x = k(y,z)$ for $(y,z) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle P,\\: Q,\\: R \\right\\rangle$ over $S$ is given by*\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left( P - Q\\dfrac{\\partial k}{\\partial y} - R\\dfrac{\\partial k}{\\partial z} \\right) \\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\n*where the unit normal to the surface is chosen such that the $\\mathbf i$-component is positive.*" :tutorial/title "Formulas for Flux Over Cartesian Surfaces"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199473 :tutorial/content "Let's calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=\\left\\langle (x-y)^2, \\:(x+y)^2, \\: 12xy \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\n \n\nthrough the surface $S$ defined by the Cartesian equation \n\n$$\nz=2y-2x\n$$\n\nfor $0 \\leq x \\leq 1$ and $0 \\leq y \\leq 1.$ \n\nFor surfaces like this, substituting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain a parametrization \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\left\\langle u,\\: v,\\: 2v-2u \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nRecall that for a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\cdot (\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2v-2u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-2⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2v-2u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\hspace{0.222em}-2,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =⟨(x-y)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}(x+y)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}12xy⟩{|}_{x=u,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,y=v,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,z=2v-2u} \\\\  & =⟨(u-v)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}(u+v)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}12uv⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and $\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨(u-v)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}(u+v)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}12uv⟩\\cdot ⟨2,\\hspace{0.222em}-2,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\  & =(u-v)^{2}\\cdot 2+(u+v)^{2}\\cdot (-2)+12uv\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨(x-y)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}(x+y)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}12xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4uv\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4uv\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2u\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}2v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =[{u}^{2}]_{0}^{1}\\cdot [{v}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =({1}^{2}-{0}^{2})\\cdot ({1}^{2}-{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
